 things that  essie can do  um so i think another cool thing that we  have built is the whole api adapter  thing that i talked about now why that  is important in this whole piece right  because if you look at the ai system in  itself there is a lot of different  moving parts it's kind of like a  choreographing um kind of a symphony  where there is systems that are in place  to ensure the apis are not abused uh  there are like you know security systems  in place that rate limit rate check and  all of that there are systems in place  that again mask sensitive information  before being processed um and then there  are systems that again interface with  data centers for the banks or the  clients now the adapter in place what it  does is it kind of makes it very  seamless to transition between these  platforms um and then you know always  kind of churn out the best kind of  experience at a very low latency i love  to hear the word easy when we're talking  about these things  so it looks like in this case federal  bank has deployments in multiple clouds  and on premise  how do you deal with latency challenges  and an implementation like this one  so latency is a big problem in this  whole system because as you can see  there is a lot of different moving parts  that's all over the place right so we  had to kind of you know design the whole  architecture with this in mind  so that every step of the way whatever  latency can be minimized however  efficient we can make the processes that  is what is done so even the milliseconds  that we can shave off each process that  is already in place um but there are  certain areas in this whole system  wherein the latency cannot be kind of  you know controlled right so for example  when you're dealing with a core bagging  transaction uh maybe someone wants to  download a historical statement spanning  last two years or three years now this  process is going to take at least two to  three minutes on the bank side so there  is no way that api is going to kind of  respond faster right so in those cases  what we have done is we have baked in  this functionality into the render  engine wherein it can decide okay this  api is notoriously slow it is going to  take a while and what it's going to do  is going to tell the customer that okay  this is going to take maybe two to three  minutes and then whenever it is ready i  can reach back to you right so two sides  whenever latency can be cut it's already  optimized and efficiently managed and  whenever we cannot really cut the  latency because the process inherently  is slow so there we kind of you know use  a human kind of a model wherein we kind  of understand always keep the customer  informed so that they don't you know get  lost in that silence  and in specifically in federal bank's  case they are running a hybrid model  wherein core infrastructure systems are  running on aws the intelligent speech is  running on gcp uh and then there is also  the data centers in place that handle  all their core banking functionalities  what about an implementation what would  it look like if a company is already  using google cloud  so if a client is already on google  cloud uh then it's a lot more easier  because a lot of our systems are already  running out of google cloud uh we can  probably like you know align the zones  even cut the latency even further right  and then think about it uh so a lot of  the ai platforms that we use uh things  like you know text to speech speech to  text dialog flow all of them are already  on gcp itself so having the entire  system move to gcp uh definitely can  actually accelerate the entire process  so if you think about uh how the system  is designed uh from our end uh primarily  the render engine piece the data magic  piece the text magic all the proprietary  components reside within gcp  and then we again communicate with  additional systems on the google side  again residing on gcp uh so if you're a  client already on google cloud uh the  integration is pretty seamless we just  bring in the system containers and then  deploy it for you and then you we can  probably get up and running  in under six weeks  any challenges that you came across  while implementing these google cloud  services and how did you overcome them  uh not really uh  we've been working with google or for  the last several years now uh it's been  pretty straightforward implementing  these things on the platform uh and  whenever we have had uh you know minor  road bumps here and there the support  team has been very proactive they've  always kind of you know helped us you  know set that thing through yeah so i  think pretty straightforward well it's  good to hear that after seven years of  working together you have a fairly  smooth experience with cloud  so now that we've covered what getting  to this point was like let's talk about  the future a little bit and talk about  what's next for wi-fi  what are the future technical  enhancements to your architecture look  like  so if you think about this whole ai  landscape where it's been rapidly  changing and there is a renewed kind of  a focus on data and  the safety of the data the privacy of  the user and all of that right now when  you think about a company that is taking  data and processing it on cloud it's  kind of like scary for people so what we  are kind of working on now is  miniaturizing the technology in order to  be able to run on people's phones right  on device processing that is where the  personalization happens  uh and probably like the anonymized  processing will still happen on the  cloud so the architecture will probably  evolve in a fashion wherein  whatever can be done on device which is  like hyper personalized for that person  will happen on their phone itself  while the massive  large scale data systems that we run  that will kind of be on the cloud  so think about a system wherein a lot of  it will be like the heavy lifting will  happen on the cloud while  personalization will happen probably  like even offline on people's phones  that is where the whole  architecture for us is heading  oh that's so cool so users will pretty  much have access to essie at their  fingers tips at almost any time so  there's a lot to look forward to there  uh where can people go to learn more  about federal bank uh so i think these  guys are the fourth largest price sector  bank in india uh so they have deployed  their assistant on their website  federalbank.com.in  um there's a case study that google did  recently  if you like to read  that's part of the whole uh how the  system was implemented for federal bank  what are the challenges that they faced  what are the kind of statistics that  went uh or like came as outcomes um and  then last most importantly if you're  like a podcast person there is also a  cool podcast that  i did along with the vp of digital uh mr  jidesh uh so again on that there is a  lot more insights in terms of how the  whole client perspective right like um i  think there's a beautiful way uh sir  puts it uh he talks about uh  do you build a house versus do you rent  a house right and then for federal bank  their entire journey was trying to build  a beautiful house that that they dreamt  of had a lot of functionality that they  wanted they wanted to work in specific  specific ways  and then how we kind of you know help  them build that out yeah so please feel  free to check all of that out  that is super cool i will definitely go  check out that podcast to hear more from  the customer and john thank you so much  again for being here and joining us to  talk about wi-fi's great ai assistant  essie and how its architecture works  with a hybrid environment or a google  cloud environment strategy to give the  best results to  in particular enterprise customers  we definitely learned a lot today and  i'm looking forward to seeing all of the  great advances that ryfai brings to the  world of ai assistants  [Mu